        "passage": "<b>THE RECOVERY OF THE NORTH ARAL SEA</b>\nStraddling the border between Kazakhstan and Uzbekistan, the Aral Sea was once the fourth-largest lake on Earth, covering some 26,000 square miles. <b>[I]</b> <u><b>Yet the story of its decline is not simply one of loss: in the north, a stubborn remnant of the sea has begun to recover.</b></u> Environmental scientists rank the lake’s collapse, which took less than three decades, among the planet’s worst ecological disasters.\nThe disaster had its origins in agricultural policy. From the 1950s onwards, Soviet planners diverted the Amu Darya and the Syr Darya, the two rivers that fed the sea, to irrigate cotton fields. <b>[II]</b> As less water reached the lake, its salinity climbed, and its abundant freshwater fish began to die out. By the 1980s the fishing industry of the Aralsk district, once a robust source of employment, had been wiped out, forcing many people to leave. Today the sea’s water surface is roughly a tenth of its original size and has split into the North and South Aral Seas; in Uzbekistan, the entire eastern basin of the South Aral Sea has completely <u><b>desiccated</b></u>, leaving only a thin strip of water in the west.\nThe scale of the loss is clear from the fishing figures. At its peak in 1957, the sea yielded more than 48,000 tons of fish, about 13 percent of the Soviet Union’s stocks. Yet as the water grew saltier, the twenty native species were decimated, and by 1987 commercial harvests had fallen to zero. <b>[III]</b> Only flounder could survive in the brine of the North Aral Sea, and the shoreline retreated until it lay about 12 miles from the town of Aralsk.\nIn the north, a different outcome was engineered. Kazakhstan, with financing largely from the World Bank, spent nearly $86 million on repairing dikes and building the Kokaral Dam, an eight-mile structure completed in the summer of 2005. <u><b>It</b></u> raised water levels by 11 feet in just seven months, a result that scientists had expected to take three years.\n<b>[IV]</b> Restoration efforts soon began to <u><b>bear fruit</b></u>. As the North Aral filled, average salinity fell from 30 grams to 8 grams per liter, which allowed almost two dozen freshwater species to return through the Syr Darya. The annual catch grew from 1,360 tons in 2006, mostly flounder, to 7,106 tons in 2016, with bream the most common, followed by roach and the prized pike-perch; the fishing limit for 2018 was set at 8,200 tons. Commerce has returned to Aralsk, where one processing plant handled around 500 tons of fish in a single year, and the village of Tastubek, which had shrunk to nine houses by the mid-1990s, now counts 34.\nProsperity, however, has brought new problems. Fishing is legally off limits during the breeding season, from May to July, but it is widespread, because fish swim closer to the shore to lay their eggs, and illicit catches are much harder for experts to track. The region’s fisheries have a history of weak management followed by the collapse of stocks. Meanwhile, a plan to raise the dam’s walls still awaits government approval. Kristopher White, a researcher who has studied the economic impact of the Aral Sea’s desiccation, cautions that the sea will never regain its former size. Nevertheless, he argues, an 18 percent increase in the North Aral Sea’s mass shows that political will and scientific research can reverse environmental damage caused by human activity.\n<i>(Adapted from National Geographic)</i>"
